  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nAttention continues to be focused on TC Isaias and the potential \nfor wind/rainfall impacts in our our forecast area from late \nFriday to Saturday. In the past 24 hrs, there was a marked \neastward/faster trend in the guidance consensus correlating with \nthe improved model initialization and data assimilation. \nUncertainty in the track (both speed and placement) is less, and\nthere is much greater probability that the center will track east\nof MS at an earlier time. Our messaging reflects this trend with \neastern portions of the area experiencing the heavier rainfall and\nstrongest winds.\n\nThere is still a reasonable chance that extratropical \ncharacteristics of the cyclone will increase as the system\napproaches landfall and weakens. This would allow for a heavier \nrainfall band with windy conditions to develop northwest/west of \nthe surface low in association with a mid level trough/low, which\nwould impact areas along/E of I-55 based on the forecast track\nfrom NHC. Recent mostly dry weather will help to limit flash \nflood concerns even with heavier overall totals, and tornado \nthreats will stay to our east. What continues to require the \ngreatest discernment is figuring out gradient wind impacts as wind\nenvelopes with the extratropical systems tend to expand even \nwhile the boundary layer wind maxima diminish.\n\nUltimately, a 12 to 18 hr period of windy/rainy conditions seems \nlike a plausible scenario for much of the area at some point in \nthe Friday night to Saturday time frame. In terms of formal \nmessaging, we continue to indicate a limited flash flood threat \nover east/ne MS, but have held off just a bit longer before \nlocking in low-end wind threats due to the changing forecast. It \nappears we may be honing in on the idea of 30 to 40 mph gust \npotential mainly east of I-55 developing by Friday evening and \ncontinuing at least until Saturday morning. This along with \nwidespread rain would certainly not be ideal for for outdoor \nevents, but the degree of disruption remains uncertain. \n\nOtherwise, it is worth noting that the subsident/well-mixed/dry\nand hot conditions ahead of the tropical cyclone could lead to an\nincrease in fire weather danger due to increased mixing and \nhot/dry/breezy conditions tomorrow and perhaps Friday as well. \nLooking ahead to beyond the system, we'll see drier and hotter \nconditions return beneath an unseasonably strong subtropical high \ncentered over our region early next week. /EC/",
